        <node id="n4::n98">
          <data key="d6">
            <y:ShapeNode>
              <y:Geometry height="284.8640943426294" width="236.97108534395738" x="10157.63689293522" y="102.88067911236703"/>
              <y:Fill color="#FFCC00" transparent="false"/>
              <y:BorderStyle color="#000000" raised="false" type="line" width="1.0"/>
              <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" autoSizePolicy="content" fontFamily="Dialog" fontSize="12" fontStyle="plain" hasBackgroundColor="false" hasLineColor="false" height="283.322265625" horizontalTextPosition="center" iconTextGap="4" modelName="custom" textColor="#000000" verticalTextPosition="bottom" visible="true" width="236.11328125" x="0.42890204697869194" xml:space="preserve" y="0.7709143588147072">Iberian cultures are discussed in their
own groupings.

The more modern Hispanic identities,
like Castilian, Asturleonese, etc. could
be considered descendents of Visigothic
and Hispano-Roman. The Visigoths,
having ruled Iberia for so long, seemed
to have referred to both the Gothic
rulers and Hispano-Roman citizens using
terms like "Hispani". Eventually, they
formed the Kingdom of Asturias after the
Umayyad conquest, which eventually split
into Leon, Asturia, Galicia, Castille, leading
to the more modern regional identities.
(The exception may be the Galicians, which
seem to have maybe began forming their
own identity before/during the time of the
original Kingdom of Asturias.)<y:LabelModel><y:SmartNodeLabelModel distance="4.0"/></y:LabelModel><y:ModelParameter><y:SmartNodeLabelModelParameter labelRatioX="0.0" labelRatioY="0.0" nodeRatioX="0.0" nodeRatioY="0.0" offsetX="0.0" offsetY="0.0" upX="0.0" upY="-1.0"/></y:ModelParameter></y:NodeLabel>
              <y:Shape type="rectangle"/>
            </y:ShapeNode>
          </data>
        </node>
