    <node id="n10">
      <data key="d6">
        <y:ShapeNode>
          <y:Geometry height="403.2871880584327" width="336.43484429895443" x="3870.858045877849" y="-1378.3117693809397"/>
          <y:Fill color="#FFCC00" transparent="false"/>
          <y:BorderStyle color="#000000" raised="false" type="line" width="1.0"/>
          <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" autoSizePolicy="content" fontFamily="Dialog" fontSize="12" fontStyle="plain" hasBackgroundColor="false" hasLineColor="false" height="386.23046875" horizontalTextPosition="center" iconTextGap="4" modelName="custom" textColor="#000000" verticalTextPosition="bottom" visible="true" width="332.16015625" x="2.137344024476988" xml:space="preserve" y="8.528359654216274">SOME NOTES ON HYBRIDIZING/DIVERGING CULTURES:

- Diverging can be done in script through the set of
'create_divergent_culture' effects. In script, you can also use
the 'can_diverge'/'can_diverge_excluding_cost` triggers to
check if a character can currently diverge their culture. This
trigger checks the scripted rule 'can_diverge_culture', and
will return true if the conditions in that rule are met. That
scripted rule also works with the hybridize/diverge culture
window the player uses. As such, if we wanted to make sure
specific divergences are done exactly when we wanted them
to be, we could modify that scripted rule with all of the specific
conditions so that we can control exactly when/where those
hybrids appear.

- For Hybridizing cultures, everything is the same as above,
but it uses the 'create_hybrid_culture' effects, the
'can_hybridize'/'can_hybridize_excluding_cost' triggers, and
the 'can_hybridize_culture' scripted rule.

- If we do something like this to limit when certain hybrids or
divergences should appear, we should just make sure that
everything else that relies on those triggers/rules isn't
messed with. As far as I know, they are used for the events
and character interaction that allows the AI to hybridize and
