        <node id="n11::n89">
          <data key="d6">
            <y:ShapeNode>
              <y:Geometry height="132.55385769536434" width="280.1284527835269" x="6121.024828247171" y="-537.3861387152796"/>
              <y:Fill color="#FFCC00" transparent="false"/>
              <y:BorderStyle color="#000000" raised="false" type="line" width="1.0"/>
              <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" autoSizePolicy="content" fontFamily="Dialog" fontSize="12" fontStyle="plain" hasBackgroundColor="false" hasLineColor="false" height="136.310546875" horizontalTextPosition="center" iconTextGap="4" modelName="custom" textColor="#000000" verticalTextPosition="bottom" visible="true" width="284.86328125" x="-2.367414233236559" xml:space="preserve" y="-1.878344589817857">Historically, it seems like there was likely a Common
Brittonic language spoken by the Brythonic peoples
before Roman Britain. After that, and especially with
the Anglo-Saxon invasion, they began diverging, and
may have become different languages by the 6th
century. It might make more sense to have them all
start under a single culture that then diverges over
time, or under certain circumstances like Rome or
Germanic invasions.<y:LabelModel><y:SmartNodeLabelModel distance="4.0"/></y:LabelModel><y:ModelParameter><y:SmartNodeLabelModelParameter labelRatioX="0.0" labelRatioY="0.0" nodeRatioX="0.0" nodeRatioY="0.0" offsetX="0.0" offsetY="0.0" upX="0.0" upY="-1.0"/></y:ModelParameter></y:NodeLabel>
